  "worked_solution": "The general solution to the equation of forced harmonic motion with light damping is the sum of the complementary function and a particular solution.\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2033}+4{y}^{\u2032}+8y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +8 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)^{2}+4 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)^{2} & =-4 \\\\ \\lambda +2 & =\\pm 2\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-2\\pm 2\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}={e}^{-2t}(P\\operatorname{cos}\u20612t+Q\\operatorname{sin}\u20612t).\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(t).$\n\nThe external force is a linear polynomial. Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a linear polynomial, i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha +\\beta t,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nSubstituting ${y}_{p}$ into the differential equation and equating coefficients gives $\\alpha =2,$ $\\beta =-2.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=2-2t,\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-2t}(P\\operatorname{cos}\u20612t+Q\\operatorname{sin}\u20612t)+2-2t.\n$$\n\nSince the particle is initially at $y=2\\,\\text{m}$ with zero velocity, the initial conditions are $y(0)=2$ and ${y}^{\u2032}(0)=0.$\n\nSolving with these conditions for $P$ and $Q$ gives\n\n$$\nP=0,\\,Q=1.\n$$\n\nSo our solution is\n\n$$\ny(t)={e}^{-2t}\\operatorname{sin}\u20612t+2-2t.\n$$\n\nTo find the position of the particle after $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ seconds, we substitute into the equation $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & ={e}^{-2(\\pi /4)}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})+2-2(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =({e}^{-\\pi /2}+2-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,\\text{m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
